# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

## Goal
Produce a PCA of the samples from gene counts, coloured by condition.

## Inputs
Project: `/tmp/run-681fbc4f/gars/projects/run-681fbc4f`.
Resolve both native artifacts again at execution time using `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`; stop if resolution fails. Paths below are relative to the project and reflect current resolution.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The design lists six samples: A1–A3 untreated and B1–B3 treated.

## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. Use Python in the existing `gars-bio` environment, with pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib; source the workspace's `_system/gars-env.sh`. Check imports in the submitted job and stop if a required package is unavailable; install nothing. All computation runs in that job.
2. Read the resolved tab-separated counts and CSV design. Use `gene_id` as the identifier and exclude `gene_name` from numeric data. Require unique, non-empty gene and sample IDs, finite non-negative counts, non-empty conditions, positive library totals, and an exact match between count columns and design sample IDs. Align columns to design order explicitly; fail on discrepancies rather than silently dropping samples. Preserve fractional counts.
3. Calculate counts per million (CPM) using each sample's total across all genes. Retain genes with CPM >= 1 in at least 3 of the 6 samples. Transform retained values with log2(CPM + 1). This is an exploratory library-size-normalised PCA; no differential-expression testing or batch correction is included.
4. Remove genes with zero variance across samples. Select up to 500 remaining genes by highest sample variance (ddof=1), breaking ties by gene_id ascending. Fail if fewer than two genes or fewer than two non-zero principal components remain. Record filtering totals and the selected genes.
5. Transpose to samples by genes and run `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=2, svd_solver="full", whiten=False)`. Centre each gene across samples without scaling to unit variance. Conditions are used only for colouring, never for fitting or selecting genes.
6. Plot PC1 versus PC2 with percent variance explained on each axis, equal axis aspect, sample IDs beside points, and a legend: untreated blue (#0072B2), treated orange (#D55E00). Save PNG at 300 dpi and PDF. Export sample coordinates joined to condition, explained variance for both components, and selected gene IDs and variances. Write a short report documenting resolved inputs, normalisation, thresholds, sample and gene totals, software versions and output filenames. Describe the PCA only; do not infer biological mechanisms or label samples as outliers.
7. Keep all scripts and results inside this analysis directory; never modify inputs. On success run the stage-03 verification helper to register declared outputs and record completion through the standard contract.

